  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nNorthwest flow with a weak trough passage across the state during \nthe early morning hours. Just a weak low level jet across the area \nat about 20kts. Given the rather nebulous setup for weak forcing \naloft and at the surface, CAMS are likewise struggling to come up \nwith a unified solution and thus will maintain a rather low chance \nfor POPs through the early morning hours. NAM BUFKIT profiles aren't \neven that unstable with minimal mid level CAPE. Then again, we \nremain in this northwest flow regime with a unidirectional shear \nenvironment so can't rule out a stronger storm or two but the \noverall prognosis is for mainly sub-severe type storms and isolated \ncoverage.  \n\nShift to this evening with a low pressure system developing out west \nand an inverted trough extending up across northeast South Dakota. A \nweak wave arrives late in the day, supporting convection in \nsoutheasterly low level flow with westerly unidirectional flow \naloft. CAMS for the most part organize into  a complex that drives \nacross central/north central South Dakota in the evening/early \novernight with an overall rapid progression east southeast across \nthe state. HREF 0-3km helicity peaks around 300m2/s2 and the 4-hour \nmax ensemble winds indicate 60+ kt potential with ensembles \nhighlighting central South Dakota. \n\nIn regards to smoke, no real change. The Pacific Northwest and \nBritish Columbia continue to generate a broad expanse of thick smoke \naloft which will continue to track across the region until we get a \npattern change. Flow around the current surface high even as it \nmoves off to the east currently has no low level smoke to advect \ninto the area.  The one impact smoke is most likely to have is \ninfluence on high temperatures. While I'll stick with NBM highs, \nrealize that initial surface based thunderstorm intensity is \ncontingent on temperatures and if the expected thick sheen of smoke \nknocks down temperatures 2-4F then the resulting convective \nevolution may not stick to the current CAM consensus. \n\nThe only major change to the pattern and forecast for the next few \ndays is a much cooler airmass coming in for the latter half of the \nwork week, as we see an eastern Canadian upper low strengthen, with \na broad high across central Canada which dribbles slightly cooler \nair into the Northern Plains.",
